+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mc2ZxQ1q3mW5bdKqsQ3WTR","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Review: FTS5 Full-Text Search\n\nVerified: 35/35 tests pass, `npm run build` and `npm run lint` clean. Implementation is `/workspace/src/search.ts` (new), `/workspace/src/db.ts` (FTS setup), `/workspace/src/routes.ts` (registration).\n\n### Overall assessment\n\nThis is a well-executed implementation. The design decisions that usually go wrong here went right: the query sanitizer is genuinely sound, the external-content triggers are correct, and route ordering is handled deliberately with a comment explaining why. I found one real bug that produces a 500, plus a latent correctness gap on file-backed databases.\n\n### What was done well\n\nThe sanitization strategy in `buildMatchQuery` is the strongest part. Rather than blacklisting FTS5 operators (the common, fragile approach), it tokenizes with `/[\\p{L}\\p{N}_]+/gu` and re-quotes each token, so no operator, quote, or bareword keyword can survive into the MATCH expression. I fuzzed it with **40,000 randomized inputs** built from FTS5 metacharacters, bareword operators (`AND`/`OR`/`NOT`/`NEAR`), unicode, and emoji, across both the plain and prefix code paths: **zero FTS5 syntax errors**. Inputs like `NEAR(a b)`, `\"unbalanced`, `col:val`, and `***` all degrade safely. The `null` return for \"nothing searchable\" is the right call — it avoids issuing a query that would error, and returns an empty result set instead. All SQL uses bound parameters; the only interpolation is the BM25 weight constants, which are module-level numeric literals, not user input.\n\nTrigger correctness is also right, which is easy to get wrong with `content=`/`content_rowid=` external-content tables. The delete and update triggers both use the `('delete', rowid, title, body)` form with the **old** column values, which is exactly what FTS5 requires to remove the correct index entries. I confirmed `INSERT INTO notes_fts(notes_fts) VALUES('integrity-check')` passes after 25 successive updates, after tag-only updates, and after deletes. Because sync lives in triggers rather than route handlers, raw SQL writes and writes from a second connection also stay indexed — I verified both.\n\n### HIGH\n\n**1. `parsePage` can produce a non-safe integer, causing a 500.** In `/workspace/src/search.ts`:\n\n```ts\nconst page = parseInt(String(raw ?? ''), 10);\nreturn Number.isFinite(page) && page > 0 ? page : 1;\n```\n\n`Number.isFinite` accepts values beyond `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`. For `?page=99999999999999999999`, `parseInt` yields `1e20`; the computed offset `2e21` is not a safe integer, and better-sqlite3 rejects it:\n\n```\nSqliteError: datatype mismatch (SQLITE_MISMATCH)\n```\n\nThis escapes as a **500 Internal server error** on a purely user-controlled parameter. Confirmed live: `GET /notes/search?q=the&page=99999999999999999999` → 500. Smaller large values (`page=1000000000000000`) correctly return 200 with an empty list, so the boundary is the safe-integer limit. Guard with `Number.isSafeInteger`, and ideally clamp the page to `Math.ceil(total / PER_PAGE)` so out-of-range pages return an empty result rather than a large useless OFFSET scan. Note this is unreachable through the current tests, which only exercise `page=1` and `page=2`.\n\n**2. Stale FTS index is never repaired on a file-backed database.** In `/workspace/src/db.ts`, the rebuild is gated on whether `notes_fts` existed before this call:\n\n```ts\nif (!alreadyIndexed) {\n  db.exec(\"INSERT INTO notes_fts (notes_fts) VALUES ('rebuild')\");\n}\n```\n\nThe clean upgrade path works — I confirmed a legacy DB with rows but no FTS table gets fully indexed on first open. But if `notes_fts` exists while the index is stale or incomplete, the rebuild is permanently skipped. I reproduced this: a DB where `notes_fts` was created but rows were inserted without triggers present leaves `MATCH 'wombat'` returning **0** forever, on every subsequent open. This is invisible in tests because they use `:memory:`, where the table never pre-exists. Since the triggers use `CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS` and the table uses `CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, a database that acquired the table without the triggers (partial/interrupted migration) is exactly this failure mode. A cheap, robust fix is to also rebuild when the index is empty but `notes` is not, or to run `'integrity-check'` and rebuild on failure.\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n**3. Prefix star is appended to a quoted phrase.** In `buildMatchQuery`, `terms[terms.length - 1] += '*'` is applied blindly. When the last term is a multi-word phrase, `?q=\"machine learn\"` produces `\"machine learn\"*`. This is valid FTS5 and does not error, but the `*` applies as a phrase-prefix to the final token only, which is subtle and probably not what the author reasoned about. Worth an explicit comment or restricting the star to single-token terms.\n\n**4. `total` is computed against `notes_fts` while results come from a JOIN to `notes`.** The count uses `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM notes_fts WHERE notes_fts MATCH ?` while `searchStmt` joins to `notes`. These agree only while the index and base table are perfectly in sync — which the triggers currently guarantee. Combined with finding 2, a stale index would make `total` disagree with the length of `results`. Counting over the same JOIN would make the two structurally consistent.\n\n**5. Suggest matches body text and returns duplicate titles.** The requirement says autocomplete returns note *titles* prefix-matched against note content. The implementation matches both columns, so `?q=acclimat` — a word appearing only in a body — returns \"Climbing Mount Kilimanjaro\", whose title contains no such prefix. This is a defensible reading of \"matches prefix against note content\" and the tests accept it, but it can surface confusing suggestions. Relatedly, two notes with the same title yield two identical entries (verified), consuming the 5-item budget; consider `SELECT DISTINCT` or grouping by title.\n\n### LOW\n\n**6. `snippet()` uses column index `-1`.** This lets FTS5 auto-select the best-matching column and is correct usage, producing proper `<mark>` output across title and body. Worth a brief comment since `-1` is non-obvious.\n\n**7. WAL pragma is a no-op for in-memory databases.** `journal_mode = WAL` silently resolves to `memory` for `:memory:` (verified). Pre-existing behavior, not introduced by this change, but it means the test suite never exercises the WAL path that production would use — which is part of why finding 2 stayed hidden.\n\n**8. Duplicate seeding on file-backed databases.** Calling `createSeededApp('/path.db')` twice inserts the seed set again (49 → 98 notes). Pre-existing behavior in `seedDatabase`, not caused by this diff, but it interacts with search: `?q=python` then returns duplicated results. Flagging for awareness only.\n\n### Untracked files\n\n`/workspace/.thunderdome-output.jsonl` and `/workspace/.thunderdome-stderr.log` are untracked and appear to be harness artifacts; `package-lock.json` is modified. None are part of the feature — worth confirming they should not be committed.\n\n### Recommendation\n\nFinding 1 is a small, contained fix and should be addressed — it is a user-triggerable 500. Finding 2 matters only if you ever run against a file-backed database, which the task explicitly asked about; the current code is correct for the fresh-file and clean-upgrade cases but not for a stale index. The remaining items are polish. The core FTS5 work — sanitization safety and trigger correctness — is solid and needs no changes."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: abc4db205ad6cde33 (use SendMessage with to: 'abc4db205ad6cde33' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 42988\ntool_uses: 32\nduration_ms: 207026</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43d1e346-7b84-459f-9add-449e61bb9053","uuid":"9414cc1c-7f48-40b1-9cb1-eeae